WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:02.501
-Ana, it is always great
to see you.

00:00:02.501 --> 00:00:03.834
You look fantastic.
You helped me out.

00:00:03.834 --> 00:00:05.133
You were on my show
"That's My Jam."

00:00:05.133 --> 00:00:06.534
-Yes, it was so fun.
-And thank you for doing that.

00:00:06.534 --> 00:00:08.200
I think you're perfect
for that show.

00:00:08.200 --> 00:00:09.701
You were, like, one of
the first people I thought of

00:00:09.701 --> 00:00:12.567
when we were doing the show,
'cause you're so great at comedy

00:00:12.567 --> 00:00:13.968
and you're so very musical.

00:00:13.968 --> 00:00:16.467
-I try.
-Did you enjoy yourself?

00:00:16.467 --> 00:00:19.067
-I loved it, 'cause I loved that
I got to be there "'SNL' Night."

00:00:19.067 --> 00:00:21.767
So I was there with
Fred and Melissa

00:00:21.767 --> 00:00:23.667
and, of course,
my beloved Uncle Dratch.

00:00:23.667 --> 00:00:24.968
-Uncle Dratch.
-Uncle Dratch.

00:00:24.968 --> 00:00:26.133
-Rachel Dratch? You call her --

00:00:26.133 --> 00:00:30.267
-Rachel Dratch, yes,
who is my work husband

00:00:30.267 --> 00:00:31.734
because we wrote that
"Clusterfunke" movie together.

00:00:31.734 --> 00:00:32.968
-That's the last time
you guys were together.

00:00:32.968 --> 00:00:33.968
-Yeah, yeah. So we came to
get together, right.

00:00:33.968 --> 00:00:35.234
-That was so funny.

00:00:35.234 --> 00:00:36.667
-So, the whole thing about
"That's My Jam" is --

00:00:36.667 --> 00:00:37.734
I have to tell you something.

00:00:37.734 --> 00:00:38.901
And this is true of
this show, too.

00:00:38.901 --> 00:00:41.868
You deliver
a quality swag product.

00:00:41.868 --> 00:00:43.868
The Jimmy Fallon brand.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:43.868 --> 00:00:46.701
-We have some good merch.
The Jimmy Fallon brand.

00:00:46.701 --> 00:00:49.300
-Yeah, so, you -- For doing --
For "That's My Jam,"

00:00:49.300 --> 00:00:51.467
which was such a delight --
and I'm not lying about this --

00:00:51.467 --> 00:00:53.734
there was a tote
that you gave us.

00:00:53.734 --> 00:00:55.133
There was a
"That's My Jam" tote.

00:00:55.133 --> 00:00:57.968
Fred and I have called each
other several times to say,

00:00:57.968 --> 00:00:59.667
"This tote is amazing."

00:00:59.667 --> 00:01:01.367
We take it to
the farmer's market.

00:01:01.367 --> 00:01:02.634
-Yeah.
-We take it to the beach.

00:01:02.634 --> 00:01:03.701
-It's a quality tote bag.

00:01:03.701 --> 00:01:05.200
-Fill it --
You take it on a plane.

00:01:05.200 --> 00:01:06.734
It's a quality tote.

00:01:06.734 --> 00:01:08.801
It had a nice -- You could put
it over the shoulder.

00:01:08.801 --> 00:01:10.067
You could take
it wherever you want.

00:01:10.067 --> 00:01:11.567
-Carry it in your hand.
-Carry it in your hand.

00:01:11.567 --> 00:01:12.767
You could --

00:01:12.767 --> 00:01:14.067
-Carry it out there like that
with weight in it

00:01:14.067 --> 00:01:15.167
and work your --

00:01:15.167 --> 00:01:16.901
-You know, but sundries.

00:01:16.901 --> 00:01:17.934
-Put sundries in there
and whatnot.

00:01:17.934 --> 00:01:19.334
-Any kind of thing.

00:01:19.334 --> 00:01:22.968
So, I love that tote so much
that I was going to knock it off

00:01:22.968 --> 00:01:26.300
when I gave my Season 2
wrap gift for "American Auto,"

00:01:26.300 --> 00:01:27.934
because you give a crew gift,
and I thought,

00:01:27.934 --> 00:01:29.467
"Well, Jimmy is classy,
and he gives classy gifts,

00:01:29.467 --> 00:01:30.701
so I'll do it."

00:01:30.701 --> 00:01:32.267
And I looked up the price.
It was so expensive.

00:01:32.267 --> 00:01:33.334
I couldn't do it.

00:01:33.334 --> 00:01:35.067
-So you didn't
give any presents at all?

00:01:35.067 --> 00:01:37.467
-No, I gave it
a much lower-grade tote.

00:01:37.467 --> 00:01:39.434
[ Laughter ]

00:01:39.434 --> 00:01:40.834
I mean, it's nice.
It said "American Auto."

00:01:40.834 --> 00:01:41.868
It looked good.

00:01:41.868 --> 00:01:42.934
And you could farmer's market --

00:01:42.934 --> 00:01:44.300
You could maybe do a
quick IKEA run.

00:01:44.300 --> 00:01:45.734
-That was a good tote.

00:01:45.734 --> 00:01:48.300
-Maybe a quick -- You could do a
quick IKEA run if you wanted,

00:01:48.300 --> 00:01:52.000
but I'm not sure the seams
could handle a Home Depot run.

00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:53.267
That's what I'm trying to say.

00:01:53.267 --> 00:01:55.734
But yours --
Your the "That's My Jam" bag --

00:01:55.734 --> 00:01:56.801
that's a Home Depot purchase.

00:01:56.801 --> 00:01:58.667
-Yeah. But through
all the years

00:01:58.667 --> 00:02:01.234
of everything you have done,
your movies and shows

00:02:01.234 --> 00:02:03.567
and things -- and you have to
have swag from different things.

00:02:03.567 --> 00:02:04.834
-So much swag.

00:02:04.834 --> 00:02:06.968
And it always makes me laugh,
because there's --

00:02:06.968 --> 00:02:09.534
It's cool, but it's also
deeply embarrassing

00:02:09.534 --> 00:02:11.968
to be seen in public
advertising your own product.

00:02:11.968 --> 00:02:13.334
You know what I mean?
-I do.

00:02:13.334 --> 00:02:15.801
-To be marching -- My husband
left the house one day.

00:02:15.801 --> 00:02:18.334
He's not conscious of
these things.

00:02:18.334 --> 00:02:20.467
He left one day for work
and he was wearing --

00:02:20.467 --> 00:02:22.501
I was in "Wicked,"
the Broadway show.

00:02:22.501 --> 00:02:25.033
[ Cheers and applause ]
Oh, yes, thank you.

00:02:25.033 --> 00:02:27.634
He left one day for work in
a "Wicked" sweatshirt,

00:02:27.634 --> 00:02:29.367
"Wicked" backpack,
and "Wicked" umbrella.

00:02:29.367 --> 00:02:31.968
Like, a completely --
And he's a big guy.

00:02:31.968 --> 00:02:33.934
-You go,
"Charlie, pick one, bud."

00:02:33.934 --> 00:02:35.501
-Pick one, and also,
like, "Wow, man.

00:02:35.501 --> 00:02:38.601
You really like Broadway."
You know?

00:02:38.601 --> 00:02:40.334
But do you remember --
I have such a fond memory,

00:02:40.334 --> 00:02:42.634
because "SNL" -- Again,
I think the great tradition of

00:02:42.634 --> 00:02:43.968
all Lorne Michaels' shows,

00:02:43.968 --> 00:02:45.634
"SNL" gives
really beautiful swag.

00:02:45.634 --> 00:02:47.634
We still have --
Because we come from the GE era.

00:02:47.634 --> 00:02:49.100
So we would get like --
-TVs.

00:02:49.100 --> 00:02:50.200
-TVs and stuff, yeah.
I mean, in the 90s.

00:02:50.200 --> 00:02:51.534
-VCRs.

00:02:51.534 --> 00:02:53.501
-I have such
a strong memory of you,

00:02:53.501 --> 00:02:55.367
because it must have been --
It was a big anniversary.

00:02:55.367 --> 00:02:57.133
It was like
the 25th or the 35th.

00:02:57.133 --> 00:02:58.434
I don't know. It was a big year.

00:02:58.434 --> 00:03:01.467
Anyway, I don't know --
somebody in charge of swag

00:03:01.467 --> 00:03:03.133
decided that what
would be a cool thing

00:03:03.133 --> 00:03:08.400
to have is
a small, black leather...

00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:09.734
-Pillow?
-...pillow.

00:03:09.734 --> 00:03:12.334
A throw pillow.
And it said "SNL."

00:03:12.334 --> 00:03:14.100
-It was a small leather pillow.
-Do you remember?

00:03:14.100 --> 00:03:17.701
-It wasn't -- It's not quite an
American Girl-doll-sized pillow,

00:03:17.701 --> 00:03:19.868
but it's like a doll pillow --

00:03:19.868 --> 00:03:22.100
-Did you keep it?

00:03:22.100 --> 00:03:24.100
-I want to say yeah.

00:03:24.100 --> 00:03:25.868
I think I still have it.
I think I still have it.

00:03:25.868 --> 00:03:28.267
-Is it in your R.V.?
Where did you --

00:03:28.267 --> 00:03:29.567
-I don't know.
Where would I put it?

00:03:29.567 --> 00:03:32.167
I don't know where -- It doesn't
go with anything I do.

00:03:32.167 --> 00:03:35.000
-I guess if you shot porn or
something in your house,

00:03:35.000 --> 00:03:36.167
then it might be cool.

00:03:36.167 --> 00:03:37.267
-I might not have it.
I might not have it.

00:03:37.267 --> 00:03:38.300
-No, I don't think it's you.

00:03:38.300 --> 00:03:39.634
You would --
That's not your brand.

00:03:39.634 --> 00:03:42.067
Like, but what I'm saying is,
I feel like that is --

00:03:42.067 --> 00:03:44.734
I'm not sure
who this is designed for

00:03:44.734 --> 00:03:46.200
and that I'm not ungrateful,

00:03:46.200 --> 00:03:48.601
because I know it cost
a lot of money.

00:03:48.601 --> 00:03:51.968
-No, we both --
-On the secondary --

00:03:51.968 --> 00:03:53.767
-Do we leave holding it?
Weren't we both, like --

00:03:53.767 --> 00:03:55.434
-I remember coming into
the hallway,

00:03:55.434 --> 00:03:58.200
and you were just creeping out
of your door like,

00:03:58.200 --> 00:04:01.234
"Did anybody else
get this pillow?"

00:04:01.234 --> 00:04:03.167
And you were very --
-We were neighbors.

00:04:03.167 --> 00:04:04.367
-We were neighbors.
We were neighbors.

00:04:04.367 --> 00:04:07.634
-We had so much fun.
-We really, really did.

00:04:07.634 --> 00:04:10.634
-Well, I want something from
"American Auto" now,

00:04:10.634 --> 00:04:12.801
because Season 2 is back.

00:04:12.801 --> 00:04:14.367
-We got key fobs.
We got cool key --

00:04:14.367 --> 00:04:15.567
Well, it's Payne Motors, right?

00:04:15.567 --> 00:04:18.000
-Payne Motors is
a great name, by the way.

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:19.234
Congrats, by the way.

00:04:19.234 --> 00:04:20.968
You know you're 100% on
Rotten Tomatoes.

00:04:20.968 --> 00:04:22.067
Like, it's a Fresh --
Certified Fresh rating.

00:04:22.067 --> 00:04:23.200
-Certified Fresh.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:04:23.200 --> 00:04:24.567
-That's a big deal.
-Thank you.

00:04:24.567 --> 00:04:28.334
-Season 1, Certified Fresh.
-Certified Fresh.

00:04:28.334 --> 00:04:30.501
I am really proud of it.
I think it's really funny.

00:04:30.501 --> 00:04:32.868
It's a great NBC
half-hour workplace comedy.

00:04:32.868 --> 00:04:34.701
-Who runs that show?
-Justin Spitzer.

00:04:34.701 --> 00:04:36.567
He created "Superstore."
-Yeah.

00:04:36.567 --> 00:04:38.100
-And he wrote on "The Office"
for 10 years.

00:04:38.100 --> 00:04:40.701
I mean, he definitely
understands, like, "the DNA."

00:04:40.701 --> 00:04:44.434
And it is a famous, non--

00:04:44.434 --> 00:04:47.000
You know, fictitious automotive
manufacturing company.

00:04:47.000 --> 00:04:49.868
And I come in as the new female
C.E.O., Katherine Hastings.

00:04:49.868 --> 00:04:51.300
And I don't drive.

00:04:51.300 --> 00:04:54.601
So -- And I come from
Big Pharma,

00:04:54.601 --> 00:04:56.400
so some gray areas
there, morally.

00:04:56.400 --> 00:04:58.234
-Yeah, it's very, very funny.

00:04:58.234 --> 00:05:00.467
-And, yeah -- And it's
a great ensemble comedy.

00:05:00.467 --> 00:05:01.901
I'm really excited about it.

00:05:01.901 --> 00:05:04.300
-I also love that you get to do
some physical comedy, too.

00:05:04.300 --> 00:05:06.434
-I guess I do get to do
some physical comedy.

00:05:06.434 --> 00:05:07.734
I don't really
think of it that way.

00:05:07.734 --> 00:05:12.634
But we're in crisis a lot
over there at Payne Motors.

00:05:12.634 --> 00:05:16.200
So our stock price
is really in the toilet.

00:05:16.200 --> 00:05:18.300
So we're really working on
getting our way back.

00:05:18.300 --> 00:05:20.000
-Yeah.
I want to show everyone a clip.

00:05:20.000 --> 00:05:21.734
Here's Ana Gasteyer in
"American Auto."

00:05:21.734 --> 00:05:23.000
Take a look at this.

00:05:23.000 --> 00:05:24.934
-It's going to be okay.
-[ Laughs ] No, no, no.

00:05:24.934 --> 00:05:26.267
It's not going to be okay. Okay?

00:05:26.267 --> 00:05:29.334
We all know it's not
going to be okay.

00:05:29.334 --> 00:05:31.334
I mean, I tell you guys
it's going to be okay,

00:05:31.334 --> 00:05:32.400
and you know why?

00:05:32.400 --> 00:05:33.868
-Uh...
-'Cause you don't lose?

00:05:33.868 --> 00:05:35.701
-No! I tell you
it's going to be okay

00:05:35.701 --> 00:05:38.601
so that you all
don't panic, okay?

00:05:38.601 --> 00:05:41.601
I mean, outside,
I'm all like, "Hey, guys,

00:05:41.601 --> 00:05:43.267
everything is
going to be just fine.

00:05:43.267 --> 00:05:45.300
Brighter days tomorrow.
Cheer up, okay?"

00:05:45.300 --> 00:05:47.033
Do you want to know what
I'm thinking inside?

00:05:47.033 --> 00:05:49.734
Would you like to know
what I am thinking inside?

00:05:49.734 --> 00:05:50.801
-Yeah, yeah.

00:05:50.801 --> 00:05:55.634
-[Bleep] [bleep] [bleep]

00:05:55.634 --> 00:05:59.234
We're all [bleep]

00:05:59.234 --> 00:06:01.701
All of us are [bleep]

00:06:01.701 --> 00:06:03.267
Alright!

00:06:03.267 --> 00:06:06.601
-Come on! That is...
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:06.601 --> 00:06:09.334
That's standing over a cliff.
-Oh, come on.

00:06:09.334 --> 00:06:10.734
-That's what I'm talking about.
That's fantastic.

00:06:10.734 --> 00:06:14.200
-I try. I try.
-Yes. Oh, my gosh.

00:06:14.200 --> 00:06:16.501
We love you.
Ana Gasteyer, everybody.

00:06:16.501 --> 00:06:19.200
"American Auto" is on NBC...

00:06:19.200 --> 00:06:22.767
-What?! Look at this tote!
Look at this tote!

00:06:22.767 --> 00:06:26.634
-"American Auto"
airs Tuesdays at 8:30 on NBC,

00:06:26.634 --> 00:06:28.601
streams on the next day
on Peacock.

00:06:28.601 --> 00:06:30.367
More "Tonight Show"
when we come back.

00:06:30.367 --> 00:06:31.467
-That is quality.
-Look at this.

